+## Concurrency Policy: Whole-Visit Revisions
 
-OPEN: select the concurrency policy before completing this node's public contract.
+Each edit is a complete visit submission tied to a base revision ID. When concurrent edits arrive from different starting states, the system retains both original submissions and flags the visit as unresolved. A coordinator compares submitted versions and selects one, marking it as the current revision.
 
-Two approaches remain credible:
+This approach trades off review workload (every competing submission creates coordinator work, even if changes are to unrelated fields) for implementation simplicity and preservation of whole-visit intent as the unit of worker action. It also keeps submission payloads straightforward — no need for structured change tracking or merge logic.
 
-- Whole-visit revisions: each edit names its starting revision. Concurrent edits produce competing complete revisions for a coordinator to resolve. This preserves the worker's submitted visit as a unit and needs relatively little merge logic, but even edits to unrelated fields create review work. Tablet-sync would retain base revision IDs and display pending competing revisions. Review-export would need a revision comparison workflow and exclude unresolved visits from its export set.
-- Field-aware three-way merge: each edit supplies its base state and changes. Disjoint field changes combine automatically; overlapping changes require review. This reduces review volume and gets more visits ready for export, but requires maintained rules for scalar fields, observer lists, and species entries. Automatically combined fields can also cross scientific relationships the rules do not understand. Tablet-sync would retain base snapshots and send structured changes. Review-export would need field-level provenance and a review view for overlapping changes.
+Original submissions are always retained; nothing is discarded.
 
-The choice trades review workload and submission size against implementation burden and preservation of whole-visit intent. Neither approach may discard original submissions. Field-aware merging is not ruled out by a requirement that every combined record receive manual approval.
+## Submission Envelope
 
-## Downstream Contract Work
+A submission carries:
 
-After selection, define the submission envelope, current-state response, unresolved-work representation, and resolution operation here. Tablet-sync will size its local storage and retry state machine around that envelope. Review-export will size its coordinator interface and export eligibility rules around the unresolved-work representation. Both must link back to this node rather than invent their own concurrency semantics.
+- `visit_id` — UUID generated by the tablet (stable across submission retries)
+- `base_revision_id` — the revision ID of the visit state the worker was editing, obtained from the last retrieval before this edit. On a fresh visit, this is null.
+- `submitted_state` — the complete visit object: plot reference, observation date, observer names, species cover list, notes
+- `author_id` — authenticated worker identifier
+- `idempotency_key` — UUID generated by the tablet to detect retries
+- `submitted_at` — client timestamp (informational; server receipt time is authoritative)
+
+## Current-State Response
+
+When a caller retrieves a visit, the response includes:
+
+- `visit_id` — the visit identifier
+- `current_revision_id` — the currently active revision (either the resolved selection from a coordinator or the only submitted revision if no conflict exists)
+- `current_state` — the complete visit object at the current revision
+- `unresolved_flag` — boolean indicating whether competing submissions exist that have not been resolved by a coordinator
+- `competing_revision_ids` — if unresolved_flag is true, the list of other submitted revision IDs so a coordinator can load them for comparison
+
+## Submission Storage and Revision Numbering
+
+Each accepted submission creates an immutable record indexed by a generated `revision_id`. The visit-level workflow progresses through revision IDs:
+
+- `current_revision_id` points to the "winning" revision — either the sole submission or the coordinator's selected resolution
+- All other submitted revisions remain stored and queryable
+- A coordinator selecting a revision makes that selection immutable; the selection identifies the winning submission ID and the other contributing submission IDs it supersedes
+
+## Review-Work Representation
+
+Unresolved review work is a visit in the unresolved state. A coordinator viewing it sees:
+
+- The visit ID and plot reference
+- The current_revision_id and current_state (the most recent or previously resolved state)
+- A list of competing_revision_ids with their full submitted states and submission metadata
+- Submission timestamps and author names for each competing submission
+
+A coordinator resolves the work by selecting one competing submission as the new current revision. This selection records the winning submission ID, the list of competing submission IDs that were not selected, the coordinator's ID, and a timestamp.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|----------|--------|------------------------|-----------|
+| Concurrency policy | Whole-visit revisions | Field-aware three-way merge | Simpler implementation: no merge rules to maintain, and review workload is more transparent to coordinators. Preserves the worker's submitted visit as a semantic unit. While more visits may need review than with field-aware merging, the two coordinators have full context in every comparison — no automatic combines that might cross unstated scientific relationships. |
+| Revision source | Immutable submitted snapshots | Computed merge results | Submitted snapshots are immutable and auditable; a coordinator resolves by selecting one, not by manually combining. This satisfies the tenet of not discarding edits and keeps the historical record clear. |
